# Settings for the throwaway GKE load-test environment (ADR-0036). Sourced by
# every script in this directory. Override any value from the environment, e.g.
#   GKE_TTL_MINUTES=90 make gke-up
#
# Nothing here is a credential. The project id is written to
# infrastructure/gke/.state (gitignored), so down.sh tears down exactly the
# environment up.sh built.

# Billing account to charge (`gcloud billing accounts list`). Required; no default.
GKE_BILLING_ACCOUNT="${GKE_BILLING_ACCOUNT:-}"

# Project. Empty (the default): up.sh creates a new project per run, named
# GKE_PROJECT_PREFIX plus a random suffix (ids are globally unique), and both
# the dead man's switch and down.sh DELETE that project. Set to the id of a
# project you created yourself to keep it: the switch then unlinks the
# project's billing instead (every charge stops, nothing is deleted), and
# down.sh deletes only what up.sh built in it, then unlinks billing too.
GKE_PROJECT="${GKE_PROJECT:-}"
GKE_PROJECT_PREFIX="${GKE_PROJECT_PREFIX:-fondaria-load}"

# Placement. up.sh picks the first region, in this order, whose quotas fit the
# cluster, and in it the first machine family with free capacity in three
# zones: it test-creates one throwaway VM per zone (no IP, no service account,
# deleted at once) because Google reports a zone out of a machine type
# (ZONE_RESOURCE_POOL_EXHAUSTED) only when a VM is actually created. The
# choice is written to .state and reused by every later run of the same
# environment. The first region also hosts the dead man's switch.
#
# A region's SSD quota is per region: a region only qualifies once its quota
# has been raised (up.sh prints the exact request when none does).
GKE_REGIONS="${GKE_REGIONS:-us-central1 us-east1 us-east4 us-west1}"
GKE_MACHINE_FAMILIES="${GKE_MACHINE_FAMILIES:-n2 n2d e2}"
# vCPUs per node, per pool (machine type <family>-standard-<n>). The stateful
# pool is sized for the demo load: a node must fit a TigerBeetle replica (1 CPU
# request) beside a Postgres instance (4 CPU, overlays/gke) or a RabbitMQ node
# (2 CPU), plus system pods, so 8 is its floor in the full profile. The
# stateless pool (and the small profile's one shared pool) stays at 4.
GKE_STATEFUL_VCPUS="${GKE_STATEFUL_VCPUS:-8}"
GKE_STATELESS_VCPUS="${GKE_STATELESS_VCPUS:-4}"
# Pin a placement instead (all of them or none; the small profile needs no
# stateful machine), e.g.
#   GKE_REGION=us-central1 GKE_NODE_ZONES=us-central1-a,us-central1-b,us-central1-f \
#   GKE_STATELESS_MACHINE=n2-standard-4 GKE_STATEFUL_MACHINE=n2-standard-8
GKE_REGION="${GKE_REGION:-}"
GKE_NODE_ZONES="${GKE_NODE_ZONES:-}"
GKE_STATELESS_MACHINE="${GKE_STATELESS_MACHINE:-}"
GKE_STATEFUL_MACHINE="${GKE_STATEFUL_MACHINE:-}"

GKE_CLUSTER="${GKE_CLUSTER:-fondaria}"
# Stable channel; the version actually installed is printed by up.sh.
GKE_RELEASE_CHANNEL="${GKE_RELEASE_CHANNEL:-stable}"

# Profile. `full` is the prod topology (60 vCPU at the defaults, ~520 GB SSD):
# 6 TigerBeetle replicas one per node. `small` fits a new billing account's default quota
# (12 vCPU, 250 GB SSD): 3 nodes, three copies of every datastore, lowered
# requests -- overlays/gke-small. Same security either way.
# Chosen once per environment and saved in .state; a later run without
# GKE_PROFILE keeps the saved one.
GKE_PROFILE_REQUESTED="${GKE_PROFILE:-}"
GKE_PROFILE="${GKE_PROFILE:-full}"

# Node pools, counts PER ZONE (three zones). full: 2 x 3 = 6 stateful nodes,
# because the prod overlay requires each of TigerBeetle's 6 replicas on its own
# node (podAntiAffinity on hostname), and 1 x 3 stateless, one per zone for the
# zone spread. gke-up creates only the stateless pool; gke-deploy creates the
# stateful one after the images are pushed, so the big machines bill only while
# the platform is actually deployed and loaded. small: one untainted pool of 1 x 3 that everything shares. The
# control plane is zonal (the free tier covers one zonal cluster's fee) and
# sits in the first node zone.
# Empty = the profile's value (full 2, small 0).
GKE_STATEFUL_NODES_PER_ZONE="${GKE_STATEFUL_NODES_PER_ZONE:-}"
GKE_STATELESS_NODES_PER_ZONE="${GKE_STATELESS_NODES_PER_ZONE:-1}"
# Boot disks on standard persistent disk: they hold only images and logs, and
# standard disk does not count against the SSD quota the data volumes need.
GKE_BOOT_DISK_TYPE="${GKE_BOOT_DISK_TYPE:-pd-standard}"
GKE_BOOT_DISK_GB="${GKE_BOOT_DISK_GB:-50}"

# Network ranges. The services range MUST contain 10.96.100.100-105: the base
# pins TigerBeetle's six replicas to those fixed ClusterIPs (base/tigerbeetle/
# service.yaml), the kind default range. GKE's default range would reject them.
GKE_SUBNET_RANGE="${GKE_SUBNET_RANGE:-10.10.0.0/20}"
GKE_PODS_RANGE="${GKE_PODS_RANGE:-10.64.0.0/14}"
GKE_SERVICES_RANGE="${GKE_SERVICES_RANGE:-10.96.0.0/16}"
GKE_MASTER_RANGE="${GKE_MASTER_RANGE:-172.16.0.0/28}"

# Dead man's switch: Cloud Scheduler deletes the whole project (or unlinks a
# kept one's billing) this many minutes after the latest `make gke-up` started,
# whether or not down.sh ever runs. Every gke-up run re-arms it from its start.
GKE_TTL_MINUTES="${GKE_TTL_MINUTES:-120}"
# Creating the stateful pool (gke-deploy) re-arms the switch to this many
# minutes from that moment: the window the big machines may bill for. Build,
# deploy, seed and six demo days fit in it; run `make gke-down` the moment the
# load finishes rather than waiting for it.
GKE_STATEFUL_TTL_MINUTES="${GKE_STATEFUL_TTL_MINUTES:-90}"

# Billing-account-wide budget alert (emails the billing admins; it does not
# stop spending). Created once, kept after the project is deleted. A budget
# must be in the billing account's own currency, which up.sh reads: USD and
# MXN accounts get these defaults; any other currency must set
# GKE_BUDGET_AMOUNT (a plain number in that currency).
GKE_BUDGET_USD="${GKE_BUDGET_USD:-25}"
GKE_BUDGET_MXN="${GKE_BUDGET_MXN:-500}"
GKE_BUDGET_AMOUNT="${GKE_BUDGET_AMOUNT:-}"
